<QuestionLayout
  title="Does the Traverse registry check that published contracts actually parse?"
  description="Since November 10, 2026 the registry publish gate parses every active contract with traverse-contracts. 25 unparseable records are deprecated with a named successor."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/does-the-registry-check-that-published-contracts-parse.html"
  category="Contracts and Capabilities"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> yes, since November 10, 2026. The registry now parses contracts with the same Rust crate Traverse uses, <code>traverse-contracts</code>, before anything publishes. Before that, it shipped records Traverse could not read.</p>

  <h2>What went wrong</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/656">registry#656</a> was found by <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1786">traverse#1786</a>. Published <code>traverse-contracts</code> 0.16.2 rejected some live records with <code>unknown variant `derived`</code>. Spec 002 allows four <code>provenance.source</code> values: <code>greenfield</code>, <code>brownfield-extracted</code>, <code>ai-generated</code> and <code>ai-assisted</code>. <code>index-v416</code> published two others:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>derived</code> on 19 records, including every version of <code>audio.transcribe-speech</code>, <code>audio.detect-speech-segments</code>, <code>report.translate-fr-semantic</code>, <code>text.detect-entities</code> and <code>text.redact-entities</code>.</li>
    <li><code>registry-wave2</code> on 6 <code>commerce.*</code> and <code>identity.*</code> records, which also lacked the required <code>author</code> and <code>created_at</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><code>traverse-cli app prepare</code> cached them, then <code>app validate</code> and <code>app register</code> failed with <code>registry_reference_requires_resolution</code>. No Traverse app could use those 25 versions.</p>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/blob/main/docs/decision-log.md">Decision 130</a> (traverse docs PR <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1831">#1831</a>) says the registry conforms to Spec 002, and Spec 002 does not change. <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/pull/657">registry#657</a> (merged November 10) implements it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Publish gate.</strong> A new bin, <code>validate_capability_contracts</code>, runs <code>traverse_contracts::parse_contract</code> on every active <code>contract.json</code> and on every one a PR adds or changes. It runs in the required <code>capability-validation</code> CI job. Spec 002 is not copied into Python.</li>
    <li><strong>24 successors.</strong> 17 <code>derived</code> records become <code>ai-assisted</code>. 2 <code>registry-wave2</code> records become <code>ai-assisted</code> with <code>author</code> and <code>created_at</code> taken from git history. The gate found 6 more failures: 4 <code>experimental</code> records patched to <code>draft</code>, <code>audio.pcm-transform@1.1.0</code> deprecated to its existing 1.1.1, and <code>core.consent-purpose-check@1.1.0</code> adding the schema wrapper. Artifacts and behavior are unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>Deprecation with a pointer.</strong> 27 new <code>deprecated.json</code> files, each naming a <code>superseded_by</code> successor. The index now writes <code>superseded_by</code> into deprecated entries. Published records are not edited.</li>
    <li><strong>Result.</strong> The gate reports 193 parsed, 0 errors.</li>
    <li><strong>Signatures.</strong> The new versions get their <code>signature.json</code> from the post-merge <code>sign-artifacts</code> job, merged as <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/pull/658">registry#658</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the live index shows</h2>
  <p>Checked on November 10 against <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/releases/tag/index-v419"><code>index-v419</code></a> (source commit <code>3d54b92</code>): 256 records, 193 active. Every active record declares a Spec 002 source (153 <code>greenfield</code>, 40 <code>ai-assisted</code>). All 25 <code>derived</code> or <code>registry-wave2</code> records are <code>deprecated: true</code> with a successor. For example, <code>audio.transcribe-speech@1.3.0</code> shows <code>superseded_by: "1.3.1"</code>, and <code>identity.principal-session@1.0.1</code> shows <code>superseded_by: "1.0.2"</code>. The fixes first published in <code>index-v417</code>.</p>
  <p>This is live registry data, not a Traverse release. Traverse is still v0.16.2.</p>

  <h2>What is not proven yet</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1830">traverse#1830</a> is still open. It will re-run the #1786 checks with published <code>traverse-cli</code> on exact pins of the five rights-backfilled successors (<code>audio.detect-speech-segments@1.2.1</code>, <code>audio.transcribe-speech@1.3.1</code>, <code>report.translate-fr-semantic@1.2.1</code>, <code>text.detect-entities@1.2.1</code>, <code>text.redact-entities@1.2.1</code>), including <code>app validate</code>. Until it lands, "the contract parses" is shown. "Your app validates on that pin" is not.</p>

  <h2>Is LLM-assisted authoring being measured too?</h2>
  <p>Yes, and the results are in. <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/pull/660">registry#660</a> fixed the method first, then <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/pull/661">registry#661</a> reported it: 7 of 25 low-risk <code>llm-assisted</code> versions escaped (28%, against a bar of 10%). Six of the seven are the parse failures this gate now catches. The seventh is a wasm32 behavior defect whose root cause is recorded as gated (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/pull/667">registry#667</a>, decision-log entry 142). The escape rate still misses the bar, so the low-risk tier stays <code>experimental</code>. Details: <a href="/questions/how-reliable-are-llm-assisted-registry-capabilities.html">How reliable are LLM-assisted capabilities in the registry?</a></p>
</QuestionLayout>
